WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:02.360
A power monitor faces a dilemma.

2
00:00:02.780 --> 00:00:05.400
Ignore the constant ripple from a converter

3
00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:09.860
yet still catch the brownout that would corrupt memory.

4
00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:13.278
Each rail is watched through its own precision divider,

5
00:00:13.278 --> 00:00:17.378
so the threshold barely moves when the temperature does.

6
00:00:17.378 --> 00:00:21.105
A fault has to last longer than 8 microseconds to count.

7
00:00:21.545 --> 00:00:24.365
Converter ripple dies inside that window.

8
00:00:24.765 --> 00:00:26.825
A real brownout survives it.

9
00:00:26.848 --> 00:00:30.668
The fault is latched and recorded, the warning pin drops first,

10
00:00:30.668 --> 00:00:32.388
and the reset follows.

11
00:00:32.728 --> 00:00:35.628
A fault cannot pass unnoticed.

12
00:00:35.628 --> 00:00:38.083
A single trimmed reference sets every threshold.

13
00:00:38.883 --> 00:00:43.903
It is adjusted once, during test, to a fraction of a percent.

14
00:00:43.903 --> 00:00:46.709
The watchdog guards the processor, not the rails.

15
00:00:47.109 --> 00:00:49.489
A kick too early, or never at all,

16
00:00:49.489 --> 00:00:52.009
and the processor restarts.

17
00:00:52.009 --> 00:00:54.088
SKU-6 is pre-silicon.

18
00:00:54.088 --> 00:00:57.808
None of these thresholds has been measured on silicon yet

19
00:00:59.068 --> 00:01:01.288
Bring us your rails and your brownout budget.

20
00:01:01.288 --> 00:01:04.428
We will tell you what SKU-6 must prove.
